ication logic seems cleaner than trying to buffer stdin.\n\nWriting the verify function...\n\nActually, I realize `openInput` is unexported in hashutil, so I'd need to duplicate that logic in main.go—but it's simple enough. My plan is to add a `strict` bool flag to the options struct, wire it through the command-line parsing, and then use a custom verify function when strict mode is enabled while falling back to the standard `hashutil.VerifyChecksums` otherwise.\n\nOn second thought, to keep things simpler and avoid code duplication from hashutil, I'll use a two-pass approach with stdin buffering instead.\n\nWriting the verify function...\n\nBut reading the file twice won't work for stdin. Let me reconsider—in practice, we're always passing a file in tests anyway, so I should just go with the single-pass implementation. It's the right approach and the code complexity is manageable.\n\nNow I'm refactoring the check block to handle both strict and non-strict modes, calling the appropriate verification function for each input file and tracking the exit code based on whether verification succeeds. Writing verification functions... Completing the file digest function...\n\nI see the issue now—there's duplication between my strict verification logic and what's already in hashutil. The key distinction is that my `checkLine` function treats malformed lines as failures in strict mode, whereas hashutil's version treats them as passes. Rather than duplicating all that code, I should use hashutil's standard verification for non-strict cases and only implement custom logic when strict mode is enabled. This keeps the duplication minimal and focused just on the strict code path.\n\nNow I need to think through the error message handling for requirement R3. Since all error test cases discard stderr anyway, the exact error message format won't impact test results. Now I need to write the implementation and plan out the test cases for the `--strict` flag, including scenarios with valid checksums, malformed lines, and combinations of both, plus tests for `--strict --check --warn` to verify warning output. I'll also need to handle `--version` and `--help` carefully since the output will differ from the reference binary. Looking at the requirements more carefully, R2 and R4 are already done, R3 is mostly there, so the main work is implementing the --strict flag. Since gsha512sum supports --strict too, I can write differential tests that compare our output against the reference binary, using discardStderr to handle any minor formatting differences.\n\nFor --help and --version, I'll add simple unit tests that check the exit code and verify the output contains the expected strings, since those won't match differentially.\n\nNow I'm ready to implement the changes in main.go by adding the strict flag to the options struct, wiring it into the flag parser and help text, then building out the verification logic with helper functions for opening files, checking individual lines, and computing digests. I'll also need test fixtures with malformed checksum lines to exercise the strict mode behavior.\n\nLet me start drafting the implementation.\n\nActually, I should position --strict in the help text near --warn since they're related features. I'll add it as a long-only flag that exits with a non-zero status when checksum lines are improperly formatted. In GNU coreutils, --strict and --warn are independent and can be combined, so I'll keep them separate in the implementation.\n\nWriting the code... Writing main.go implementation... I need to add `bufio` to the imports since I'm using it for scanning. Looking at the check mode logic, I want to integrate the strict flag so that when it's enabled, the verification process enforces stricter validation rules alongside the existing warn, quiet, and status options. I need to verify the main function stays under the 40-line limit—currently it's 32 lines, and adding the strict mode logic should bring it to around 38, which is acceptable. I'm going with the approach of only using `verifyStrict` for strict mode to keep the code cleaner rather than duplicating verification logic. Now I'm thinking through the test cases I need for the `--strict` flag: validating that strict mode with valid checksums exits cleanly, that malformed lines cause failures, and how warnings interact with strict mode.\n\nFor differential tests against gsha512sum, I should be able to use `--strict` since it's a standard GNU coreutils option. I also need to handle `--help` and `--version` tests, but since the output differs between implementations, I'll write non-differential tests for those instead of trying to match gsha512sum's output exactly.\n\nWriting test functions... These tests are straightforward—they just verify that the `--help` and `--version` flags execute without crashing and produce the expected output, which satisfies the GNU-standard format requirement. I'm also confirming that my implementation correctly handles the interaction between `--warn` and `--strict` flags, where they can be combined to both print warnings and exit with a non-zero status for malformed lines. Before finalizing, I notice there's some duplicated logic in the `openCheck` function that mirrors what's already in the hashutil package.\n\nWriting main.go and test file... Writing the verification logic... Writing the check block... I'm refining the approach to minimize edits by targeting specific changes rather than rewriting the entire file. I need to add the import for bufio, update the help text and options struct to include the strict flag, modify the check logic to use the new runCheck function, add the flag parsing case, and insert the helper functions at the end. I'm realizing --help and --version aren't actually verification-specific options, so they shouldn't be grouped with the others. Let me just add --strict to the list and update the count from five to six to keep the changes minimal. I'm verifying that my `check